<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Fante Confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">1868 CE – 1873 CE · capital Mankessim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An alliance of Fante states on the Gold Coast that drafted a modern written constitution in 1871, an early landmark of African self-government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1867-1868 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> exchanged forts on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a>, placing some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> towns under Dutch control, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> threatened invasion. Fante kings and educated Fante met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mankessim">Mankessim</a> in 1868 and formed a confederacy to coordinate defence and resist the transfer, besieging Dutch-allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1871 the confederacy adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mankessim-constitution">Mankessim Constitution</a>, which provided for a king-president, an executive council, an assembly of kings and chiefs, a poll tax, schools, roads and the development of agriculture and mining. Its leaders included the king-president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghartey-iv">Ghartey IV of Winneba</a> and Western-educated men such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-hutton-brew">James Hutton Brew</a>. It sought cooperation with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> but also self-government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British saw the confederacy as a challenge, arrested its leaders in 1871 and refused recognition. It faded by 1873, and in 1874 the area became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a>. Ghanaian nationalists later saw it as a forerunner.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1868 CE – 1873 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path